              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "When your cat grabs a toy with its front paws, rolls onto its side or back, and furiously thumps its hind legs against it, they are performing a behavior commonly known as the **\"bunny kick\"** (or \"rabbit kick\"). \n\nWhile it looks incredibly cute, the bunny kick is actually a deeply ingrained, highly coordinated physical behavior. Here is why your cat does it:\n\n### 1. It is a Hunting Instinct (The \"Kill Kick\")\nAt their core, domestic cats are still fine-tuned predators. In the wild, when a cat catches a medium-to-large sized prey animal (like a rabbit, bird, or large rodent), they will latch onto it with their front paws and teeth to secure it. They then roll onto their side and use their incredibly powerful hind legs to rapidly rake and kick. \nIn the wild, this rapid kicking serves to quickly disable, stun, or disembowel the prey so it cannot fight back. When your cat does this to a toy, they are practicing their ancestral hunting skills and \"killing\" their play-prey.\n\n### 2. It is a Self-Defense Maneuver\nWhile we often view a cat lying on its back as a sign of vulnerability or an invitation for a belly rub, for cats, it is actually a highly effective defensive fighting posture. \nIf a cat is cornered or fighting a larger predator, rolling onto their back allows them to bring all of their weapons into play at once: their teeth, their front claws, and\u2014most importantly\u2014their sharp hind claws. When bunny kicking, they are practicing how to ward off a threat and defend their vulnerable belly.\n\n### 3. Play, Exercise, and Stress Relief\nCats have a lot of pent-up physical and mental energy, especially indoor cats. Bunny kicking requires a massive amount of physical exertion and engages their largest core and leg muscles. Doing this to a toy is a fantastic way for them to burn off excess energy, relieve stress, and get a great workout.\n\n### 4. Overstimulation\nSometimes, cats will bunny kick when they become overstimulated. If you are playing with your cat or petting them, and they suddenly grab your hand/arm and start bunny kicking you, it usually means they have crossed the threshold from relaxed play into high-arousal or defensive mode. \n\n---\n\n### What should you do?\n* **Encourage it with \"Kicker\" toys:** Because this is a natural, healthy instinct, it is great to encourage it! You can buy long, cylindrical stuffed toys called **cat kickers**. These are designed specifically to be hugged and kicked, saving your furniture (and your hands) from their claws.\n* **Do not use your hands as toys:** If your cat tries to bunny kick your hand or arm, **do not pull away quickly**, as this mimics fleeing prey and will make them grab on tighter and kick harder. Instead, freeze, wait for them to loosen their grip, and calmly substitute your hand with a kicker toy or a wand toy to redirect their energy.",
